            <ArticleTitle>Correlation of calcium measurement with low dose 64-slice CT and angiographic stenosis in patients with suspected coronary artery disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>249-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijcard.2008.11.043</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Coronary artery calcium is a sensitive marker of coronary artery disease (CAD). This study was to determine the relationship between coronary calcium score (CCS) and angiographic stenosis on a patient-based or vessel-based analysis. 91 consecutive patients underwent both low dose 64-slice CT calcium scoring scan and conventional angiography of the heart. The total CCS of abnormal coronary angiogram (n=45) was 297.38+/-416.93, whereas that of normal coronary angiogram (n=46) was 5.37+/-9.35 (P&lt;0.001). The CCS and degree of stenosis were moderately correlated on patient-based or vessel-based analysis (r=0.517, 0.521, respectively; both P&lt;0.001). In conclusion, CCS could reflect the degree of vessel stenosis to some extent, but CCS of zero could not rule out CAD.</AbstractText>
